# Test CVs (contain real personal data)
Test Cvs/

# Implementation documentation — local only. The authoritative spec docs,
# but the newer research docs (16, 17) capture live reproduction runs
# against real CVs and job posts, including personal data, and the pack
# otherwise churns with every working session. Was previously tracked, so
# this rule alone doesn't untrack it; `git rm -r --cached "Implementation
# pack"` removed it from the index, keeping the files on disk.
Implementation pack/

# Temp files
temp_metrics.txt

# Raw ESCO source export (~760MB) — one-time input to
# backend/scripts/ingest_esco.py, not a repo artifact. The generated
# output (backend/app/data/skills_index.json, ~5MB) is what's committed.
ESCO dataset*/
ESCO dataset*.zip

# Reference implementation checked out for side-by-side comparison, not a
# part of this project. It carries its own .git, .replit and node_modules,
# so committing it would nest a foreign repo inside this one. The parts we
# actually adopted were copied to backend/extraction-service/ (tracked) and
# are documented in backend/decommissioned/README.md.
Example/

# Old extraction pipeline (Docling/Textract/merge/cv_parse), kept verbatim
# on disk for local read/diff/restore per its own README, but not something
# a fresh clone or collaborator needs — was previously tracked, so this
# alone won't stop git from seeing it; see backend/decommissioned/README.md.
backend/decommissioned/

# Diagnostic scratchpad from the Example-vs-JBS comparison. The raw JSON
# both systems returned for one CV/JD pair is evidence for a decision, not
# code the app runs. The three analysis docs alongside it were already
# committed and stay committed, so this ignores entries individually and
# re-includes them by name — a bare `CV matching fix/` would exclude the
# directory, and git cannot re-include a file whose parent dir is excluded.
CV matching fix/*
!CV matching fix/bug-report.md
!CV matching fix/cv-diagnosis.md
!CV matching fix/essay-diagnostic-methodology.md